anv/xemac: VK_MEMORY_UNMAP_RESERVE_BIT_EXT leaves the range reserved without a hole in between

anv_device_unmap_bo(replace = true) took the BO mapping down (xemac_munmap) and then mmap(MAP_FIXED, PROT_NONE) over
the range it had left unmapped. On macOS 15 an mmap(MAP_FIXED) over an unmapped gap kills the process: EXC_GUARD,
GUARD_TYPE_VIRT_MEMORY, DEALLOC_GAP at the placed address (vkplaced, in anv_UnmapMemory2). With replace, xemac now does
both in one step (xemac_munmap_reserve: the placed view is overwritten in place with a PROT_NONE reservation, so the
range is never a hole). Needs the xemac of xemac-placed-map-mach_vm_remap.patch (second version).

diff --git a/src/intel/vulkan/anv_allocator.c b/src/intel/vulkan/anv_allocator.c
index 6c811f94761..e503e462d18 100644
--- a/src/intel/vulkan/anv_allocator.c
+++ b/src/intel/vulkan/anv_allocator.c
@@ -1929,7 +1929,18 @@ anv_device_unmap_bo(struct anv_device *device,
    VG(VALGRIND_FREELIKE_BLOCK(map, 0));
 
 #ifdef HAVE_XEMAC
-   /* BO mappings are the kernel extension's; it takes them down. */
+   /* BO mappings are the kernel extension's; it takes them down. With
+    * replace, it leaves the range reserved itself: unmapping first and then
+    * mmap(MAP_FIXED) over the hole is killed with EXC_GUARD (DEALLOC_GAP) on
+    * macOS 15.
+    */
+   if (replace) {
+      if (xemac_munmap_reserve(map, map_size) != 0) {
+         return vk_errorf(device, VK_ERROR_MEMORY_MAP_FAILED,
+                          "Failed to map over original mapping");
+      }
+      return VK_SUCCESS;
+   }
    xemac_munmap(map, map_size);
 #endif
    if (replace) {
